      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by finding her walking rate in miles per minute. We know that Jana takes 24 minutes to walk 1 mile. Therefore, her rate of walking is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Rate} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes. We do this by multiplying her rate by the time she walks:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo express this as a decimal, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} \\approx 0.416667\n\\]\n\nRounding this to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\n\\[\n0.416667 \\approx 0.4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
